00:27:48,640 --> 00:27:55,640
May those of us who maybe have never understood you to be father understand that and experience that for the first time.

261
00:27:55,640 --> 00:28:05,640
And for those of us, God, that know and love you as father, but are easily discouraged, easily filled with shame, guilt, fear, anxiety.

262
00:28:05,640 --> 00:28:11,640
God, may your fatherly love cover us.

263
00:28:11,640 --> 00:28:18,640
May we be reminded that our father is the father who owns a 10,000 cattle on a hill.

264
00:28:18,640 --> 00:28:22,640
God, everything is yours and you are provider for us.

265
00:28:22,640 --> 00:28:25,640
And so there's nothing that we need to be afraid of.

266
00:28:25,640 --> 00:28:26,640
We love you.

267
00:28:26,640 --> 00:28:28,640
We thank you for this day.

268
00:28:28,640 --> 00:28:29,640
It's in Jesus name I pray.

269
00:28:29,640 --> 00:28:30,640
Amen.

270
00:28:30,640 --> 00:28:43,640
We've been looking at this passage in Isaiah chapter nine, verses six and seven that give us a few titles or names of what Isaiah was preparing God's people to expect when Messiah comes.

271
00:28:43,640 --> 00:28:44,640
And this is what it says.

272
00:28:44,640 --> 00:28:48,640
It says for a child is born to us.

273
00:28:48,640 --> 00:28:50,640
A son is given to us.

274
00:28:50,640 --> 00:29:01,640
When Messiah comes, the government will rest on his shoulders and he will be called wonderful counselor, mighty God, everlasting father, prince of peace.

275
00:29:01,640 --> 00:29:04,640
His government and its peace will never end.

276
00:29:04,640 --> 00:29:09,640
He will rule with fairness and justice from the throne of his ancestor David for all eternity.

277
00:29:09,640 --> 00:29:16,640
The passionate commitment of the Lord of heaven's armies will make this happen.

278
00:29:16,640 --> 00:29:24,640
So we've been looking at these titles and many of them make sense on the surface from what we know about Jesus from the New Testament.

279
00:29:24,640 --> 00:29:29,640
Some of them, there's tension between what he brought at his first coming and what he will bring at his second coming.

280
00:29:29,640 --> 00:29:38,640
This passage very much is an already not yet passage, but I don't know about you, but the one that sticks out to me and maybe is the most perplexing to me is that of everlasting.

281
00:29:38,640 --> 00:29:48,640
Father, because I've grown up with the New Testament in my hands and the New Testament describes Jesus, our Messiah as the Son of God.

282
00:29:48,640 --> 00:29:52,640
So how can he be both son and father?

283
00:29:52,640 --> 00:30:02,640
Well, lucky for you, we do not have time to do a, to unpack Trinitarian, Trinitarian theology.

284
00:30:02,640 --> 00:30:07,640
And even if we did, we would likely walk away more confused than we walked in with.

285
00:30:07,640 --> 00:30:19,640
Because the reality is, I think this idea of Trinity, God, God, the father, the son and the Holy Spirit is meant to be a mystery that we will never fully grasp and that we are invited to marvel in.

286
00:30:19,640 --> 00:30:23,640
So I don't have time to fully kind of unpack what all of this looks like.

287
00:30:23,640 --> 00:30:29,640
And even if I did, I don't know that I fully grasp it with my small brain.

288
00:30:29,640 --> 00:30:32,640
And that's not a, yeah, anyway.

289
00:30:32,640 --> 00:30:51,640
What I do have time to say is that when Jesus shows up as Emmanuel, when he shows up as God with us, the promised Messiah from the beginning pages of Scripture, described to us in this passage of Isaiah, what he does do, I believe, is he demonstrates and teaches us what the everlasting father is like.

290
00:30:51,640 --> 00:31:03,640
That this is his role as Messiah, that when he comes as the word in the flesh dwelling among us, that he both demonstrates for us what the father is like in his characteristics, in his nature, in his qualities.

291
00:31:03,640 --> 00:31:12,640
As we read stories about Jesus, we are reading stories about what the father would do if he was living on a sinful earth.

292
00:31:12,640 --> 00:31:29,640
He demonstrates for us what the father is like, and he clarifies by his teachings for us what the father is like, because it's easy for us to be confused and to misunderstand and to read some passages from the Old Testament and say, I don't think the father is loving, right?

293
00:31:29,640 --> 00:31:32,640
So this is what Jesus does for us as the Messiah.

294
00:31:32,640 --> 00:31:36,640
As he comes, he demonstrates and he teaches what the everlasting father is like.

295
00:31:36,640 --> 00:31:45,640
First, he demonstrates and as a pastor, I have the privilege of talking to a lot of people that are skeptical about Christianity.

296
00:31:45,640 --> 00:31:52,640
And I'm sure you all have had conversations with friends and family who maybe struggle with the idea of Christianity or the Bible.

297
00:31:52,640 --> 00:32:05,640
And I don't know about you, but one of the overarching themes that comes back to me over and over again with people who struggle with Christianity is they see a disconnect between the God they read about in the Hebrew Bible or the Old Testament and the Jesus of the New Testament.

298
00:32:05,640 --> 00:32:18,640
And as someone who just spent some time reading through the Old Testament again over the last few months, I can be honest and say I resonate with some of their tensions.

299
00:32:18,640 --> 00:32:21,640
I often feel this tension as well.

300
00:32:21,640 --> 00:32:35,640
However, I think oftentimes the tension that we have is because we misunderstand what's happening in the Old Testament or oftentimes God's people in the Old Testament do things in God's name that he never commanded or condoned.

301
00:32:35,640 --> 00:32:38,640
And so God gets the credit for Israel's failures.

302
00:32:38,640 --> 00:32:44,640
And people look at God in the Old Testament and Jesus in the New Testament and they get confused.

303
00:32:44,640 --> 00:32:50,640
But what I want to remind us of is this conversation that Jesus has with his disciples, particularly Philip.

304
00:32:50,640 --> 00:32:53,640
Philip asks him this question.

305
00:32:53,640 --> 00:32:57,640
He says, "Lord, show us the Father and that will be enough for us."

306
00:32:57,640 --> 00:32:59,640
We just want to see God.

307
00:32:59,640 --> 00:33:00,640
We want to see the everlasting Father.

308
00:33:00,640 --> 00:33:01,640
We want to see what he's like.

309
00:33:01,640 --> 00:33:05,640
We want to see who he is and that will be enough for us.

310
00:33:05,640 --> 00:33:10,640
We won't be afraid and we can be confident in the mission that you're giving us.

311
00:33:10,640 --> 00:33:20,640
And Jesus responds, "Don't you know, Philip, even after I've been among you such a long time, anyone who has seen me has seen the Father."

312
00:33:20,640 --> 00:33:41,640
This is the proclamation of the New Testament, that Jesus and the Father are one, that when you see Jesus interacting with the marginalized, with the sinful, with the broken, with the sick, that when you see Jesus showing compassion and grace, this is the activity of the Father from all time.

313
00:33:41,640 --> 00:33:46,640
Nothing, there wasn't some flip of the script where God decided to change his character when he sent Jesus.

314
00:33:46,640 --> 00:33:53,640
This is what God is like when we see Jesus extending grace and compassion and patience.

315
00:33:53,640 --> 00:34:00,640
We see the everlasting Father who created us out of love and invites us into a relationship with him.

316
00:34:00,640 --> 00:34:06,640
When we see him confronting the self-righteous and the religious leaders, but doing so with compassion.

317
00:34:06,640 --> 00:34:09,640
I love this about Jesus, because we want to talk about Jesus flipping tables.

318
00:34:09,640 --> 00:34:24,640
But when you watch the conversations that Jesus has with these religious leaders, he's constantly brokenhearted and inviting them to see where they've made mistakes, where they've misunderstood who the Father is, where they're acting out of religious duty rather than relational identity.

319
00:34:24,640 --> 00:34:26,640
And he's saying, "You're missing the point.

320
00:34:26,640 --> 00:34:27,640
Come back to the Father.

321
00:34:27,640 --> 00:34:29,640
Understand what the Father is inviting you to."

322
00:34:29,640 --> 00:34:33,640
Even when we see him confronting and challenging, he does so with compassion.

323
00:34:33,640 --> 00:34:42,640
And these are the attributes of God, the everlasting Father, lived out through the flesh of humanity in Jesus.

324
00:34:42,640 --> 00:34:52,640
God, the everlasting Father, who is one with Jesus the Messiah, sees every human being as his child created in his image and desires a relationship with each of them.

325
00:34:52,640 --> 00:34:54,640
That is the story of the Bible.

326
00:34:54,640 --> 00:35:01,640
The story of the Bible is that God wants a relationship with you.

327
00:35:01,640 --> 00:35:04,640
However, many of us, all of us at birth have rejected that relationship.

328
00:35:04,640 --> 00:35:06,640
We've walked away from him.

329
00:35:06,640 --> 00:35:15,640
And the Scriptures resounding message over and over and over again is this repetitive invitation to come back to the Father.

330
00:35:15,640 --> 00:35:21,640
That God is moving towards us with his grace, even when we are moving away from him in rebellion.

331
00:35:21,640 --> 00:35:31,640
This is the character of our everlasting Father demonstrated to us in the person of Jesus.

332
00:35:31,640 --> 00:35:39,640
So this is the first thing I think that Isaiah means when he says the Messiah will be everlasting Father, that he will demonstrate to us what the Father is like.

333
00:35:39,640 --> 00:35:44,640
We will be able to see the activity of the Father in the flesh.

334
00:35:44,640 --> 00:35:54,640
And the second thing he does is not only does he demonstrate it for us, he gives us an example, but he teaches us what the everlasting Father is like.

335
00:35:54,640 --> 00:36:08,640
One of Christ's most masterful teachings, in my opinion, is in Luke chapter 15, where he gives this very descriptive and illustrated story of what God is like as our everlasting Father.

336
00:36:08,640 --> 00:36:26,640
And again, I don't know about you, but one of the other issues that people both inside and outside the church often bring to me about this idea of God as everlasting Father is many of us have had complicated relationships with our earthly Father.

337
00:36:26,640 --> 00:36:37,640
And so when we take this idea of God being everlasting Father and connected with the image that we have of Father, it really has this tendency to create tension.

338
00:36:37,640 --> 00:36:50,640
I know that there are some of us in this room who have been abandoned by our fathers, who have been physically or emotionally abused by our fathers, left with a number of unmet expectations.

339
00:36:50,640 --> 00:36:54,640
That is the reality of fallen and broken creation.

340
00:36:54,640 --> 00:37:02,640
And even those of us who had great relationships with our dads have been hurt and disappointed by them at some point in our lives.

341
00:37:02,640 --> 00:37:14,640
And so what Jesus does when he comes to teach what the Father is like is he gives us a story that is meant to help us reimagine what it looks like for God to be Father.

342
00:37:14,640 --> 00:37:25,640
He wants us to kind of brush away any potential image we have of earthly fathers and he wants us to reimagine what it actually means for God to be Father.

343
00:37:25,640 --> 00:37:29,640
And he does so by telling a story of a good Jewish father.

344
00:37:29,640 --> 00:37:48,640
The story in what is known oftentimes as the parable of the prodigal son is a story of a essentially a good father in an honor shame culture, a father who in this culture would have been expected to protect and care for their children as long as the children followed the rules.

345
00:37:48,640 --> 00:37:56,640
And then what happens when the children follow the rules is the dad is released from his responsibility to take care of the children.

346
00:37:56,640 --> 00:38:03,640
And what Jesus does in this story is he flips even the image of a good father, he flips it upside down.

347
00:38:03,640 --> 00:38:14,640
And he says that not only is God the Father a good father by earthly standards, but he's so much more than that.

348
00:38:14,640 --> 00:38:15,640
He flips the script.

349
00:38:15,640 --> 00:38:20,640
And when he is dishonored, he actually dishonors himself in order to honor us.

350
00:38:20,640 --> 00:38:28,640
That this is the the thing that Jesus is trying to do for us is give us a beautiful vision of what it actually looks like for God to be father.

351
00:38:28,640 --> 00:38:33,640
Like I said, the story is most well known as the parable of the prodigal son.

352
00:38:33,640 --> 00:38:38,640
But as I've studied more, I actually prefer to call it the parable of the compassionate father.

353
00:38:38,640 --> 00:38:44,640
Because I think the more I look into this story, the more I look into what Jesus was all about.

354
00:38:44,640 --> 00:38:52,640
Is that the premise, the idea, the whole focal point of the story is actually about the action of the father.

355
00:38:52,640 --> 00:39:05,640
I actually heard somebody say this week that you could essentially like, essentially, he said that the characters of the sons are almost exchangeable.

356
00:39:05,640 --> 00:39:13,640
Like Jesus chose a prodigal son and a self-righteous son because those were the two people in the audience that he was speaking to.

357
00:39:13,640 --> 00:39:18,640
He was talking to the tax collectors and sinners and he was talking to the religious leaders.

358
00:39:18,640 --> 00:39:24,640
And so he used these two images of sons because that was best represented of the category of people in the room.

359
00:39:24,640 --> 00:39:33,640
But you could exchange the characters of the sons with any type of rebellion, any type of walking away from God, any type of dishonoring God.

360
00:39:33,640 --> 00:39:38,640
And what doesn't change in the parable is the character of the father.

361
00:39:38,640 --> 00:39:45,640
That no matter what type of sin or rebellion you do, so maybe you don't necessarily fall into a category of a prodigal or self-righteous.

362
00:39:45,640 --> 00:39:48,640
But at some point in time, you have rebelled against the father.

363
00:39:48,640 --> 00:39:50,640
You have dishonored him.

364
00:39:50,640 --> 00:39:57,640
And what does not change in this story is that God is a compassionate father who's reaching out to you with relationship.

365
00:39:57,640 --> 00:40:02,640
That he's willing to do what it takes to fix the brokenness of the relationship.

366
00:40:02,640 --> 00:40:09,640
The driving characteristic of the father in this story is that of compassion to his wayward children.

367
00:40:09,640 --> 00:40:20,640
There's this word in the story of the parable of the prodigal son that when the son is far off, it says the father saw him and had compassion on him.

368
00:40:20,640 --> 00:40:28,640
We talked about this word when we did the parable of the good Samaritan, the Greek word is splachizomai.

369
00:40:28,640 --> 00:40:35,640
I can't believe I remembered that because I don't have it in my notes and I wasn't planning on sharing it, but there it is.

370
00:40:35,640 --> 00:40:45,640
It is a word that is only ever used of Jesus and of the Jesus characters in his parables.

371
00:40:45,640 --> 00:40:48,640
It is this gut wrenching gut level.

372
00:40:48,640 --> 00:40:53,640
I'm going to do whatever it takes to fix the thing, the situation that this person is in.

373
00:40:53,640 --> 00:40:59,640
I have compassion on them and I am reaching out to them and I will do whatever it takes to fix this situation.

374
00:40:59,640 --> 00:41:09,640
So Jesus takes the image of a good Middle Eastern father in an honor shame culture who would lovingly protect and provide for his children as long as they complied with cultural expectations.

375
00:41:09,640 --> 00:41:11,640
And he flips it on his head.

376
00:41:11,640 --> 00:41:18,640
In this story, the father is dishonored by his sons and would be fully justified by cultural expectations to cut them off from relationship.

377
00:41:18,640 --> 00:41:23,640
But instead he dishonors himself in order to protect and redeem his children.

378
00:41:23,640 --> 00:41:28,640
Jesus is giving us a teaching on what it means for God to be our everlasting father.

379
00:41:28,640 --> 00:41:37,640
So what I want us to think about this morning is whatever baggage you come into this place with when it comes to this title of father.

380
00:41:37,640 --> 00:41:45,640
I'm inviting you and I think Jesus is inviting you both by his actions, his activity and his teachings.

381
00:41:45,640 --> 00:41:53,640
He's inviting you to lay that baggage down and begin to see a clearer image of what it means for God to be your everlasting father.

382
00:41:53,640 --> 00:42:08,640
Who is filled with compassion towards you who will do whatever it takes to protect and redeem you even as you are actively rejecting him.

383
00:42:08,640 --> 00:42:18,640
So as we begin to kind of conclude our time here, I want to end our time together by reading a modern day version of this parable of the compassionate father.

384
00:42:20,640 --> 00:42:25,640
If we don't have tissues out, we might want to get them.

385
00:42:25,640 --> 00:42:30,640
It's in a book written by Philip Yancey called What is So Amazing About Grace.

386
00:42:30,640 --> 00:42:45,640
And his goal here is to help us just lay our baggage of what we think about God as father at the foot of the cross and reimagine what it might look like in modern day setting for God to be a God of compassionate, of a compassionate father.

387
00:42:45,640 --> 00:42:57,640
And one of the reasons I chose this story today as we host Watts is that in the midst of this story, the character ends up living on the streets.

388
00:42:57,640 --> 00:43:04,640
And so when we think about our guests and the people that we are welcoming into our home,

389
00:43:04,640 --> 00:43:14,640
I just want us to realize that there are a million different ways that we might find ourselves in situations that we find ourselves in.

390
00:43:14,640 --> 00:43:20,640
There are, we might make a few decisions when we're young and not have a support system.

391
00:43:20,640 --> 00:43:23,640
We might have bad things hit us over and over again.

392
00:43:23,640 --> 00:43:28,640
There's a number of different ways that we might find ourselves in need.

393
00:43:28,640 --> 00:43:33,640
And what God wants to do is extend his loving hand towards us as everlasting father.

394
00:43:33,640 --> 00:43:50,640
So my hope is that this emotional and imaginative story about the compassion of God as everlasting father told in a modern day narrative will help us better grasp how high and how wide and how deep the father's love is for us.

395
00:43:51,640 --> 00:43:55,640
If you're really an imaginative person and you want to close your eyes, you can.

396
00:43:55,640 --> 00:43:58,640
Again, I'm reading this directly from Philip Yancey's book.

397
00:43:58,640 --> 00:44:00,640
What's so amazing about grace?

398
00:44:00,640 --> 00:44:06,640
A young girl grows up on a cherry orchard just outside Traverse City, Michigan.

